 *
 * 1. Compare digests, not tokens. A digest is a fixed 64 characters, so the comparison's
 *    cost cannot vary with the secret's length. Comparing raw tokens would leak the token
 *    length through timing, and would make the length of the configured token observable.
 * 2. No early exit. The comparison walks all 64 characters and accumulates differences,
 *    so it takes the same time whether the mismatch is in the first character or the last.
 *    A `===` on two strings returns as soon as it finds a difference, which turns the secret
 *    into something an attacker can extract one character at a time.
 * 3. Every slot is always checked. Rotation keeps two hashes live. Both are compared even
 *    once one has matched, so which slot a token belongs to is not observable either — and,
 *    more importantly, so that nobody later "optimises" the loop into a short circuit.
 *
