The error table. For OAuth I need:
- Callback query missing code/state → `MalformedAuth`? MalformedAuth is "authorization header did not parse" — title mismatch. Better a new code: `MalformedOAuth` (400) "OAuth callback did not parse"? Hmm — but what title? "the OAuth callback query did not parse". 
- Unknown/expired state → `Unauthorized`? It's "missing or invalid credential" — state isn't a credential exactly but semantically the flow's credential. Plan says "fail closed on miss". A dedicated code `OauthStateUnknown` (401) "OAuth state unknown or expired" is clearer in logs and matches the plan document's vocabulary ("single-use oauth state, replay-refused callback"). New codes are allowed to be added.
- GitHub exchange failure (non-200 from access_token endpoint or bad JSON) → `AuthUnavailable` (backend outage class; the 503 retry semantics) ✓ existing.
- Login succeeded but not org member → `ForbiddenOrg` (403) ✓ existing! Perfect fit — the verdict path uses Unauthorized for non-members... wait, verdict returns Unauthorized for non-members. Hmm, inconsistent? For OIDC tokens: valid token outside the org = ForbiddenOrg 403. For device verdicts: non-member = 401 unauthorized. For browser: pick 403 ForbiddenOrg — a VALID GitHub login outside the org is exactly "a valid credential from outside the configured org" = the OIDC semantics. But the verdict path maps denied membership to 401... that's for tokens whose /user check fails (401 from GitHub = invalid token) vs actual non-members (verdict org_member=false with valid login). Both map to 401 there. Hmm.

For sessions, keep it simple and mirror the verdict path's behavior for consistency within the browser flow: failed org check → 401 Unauthorized? But then the later SPA shows "unauthorized"... The plan: "checks GET /user + org membership" → on failure the callback refuses. Code choice: ForbiddenOrg is more precise when the user is valid but not a member — same semantics as OIDC. However the READ path refuses non-member tokens with 401. For the callback I'll use ForbiddenOrg (the token verified, the org gate refused) — defensible and specific; the threat model names "org gate enforced".

- Exchange endpoint reachable but answers an error (bad code) → that's not an outage; GitHub answers 200 with {error: "bad_verification_code"} typically! OAuth web errors come as 200 + error payload. So: bad code → 401? Map error payload → Unauthorized (the code didn't verify). Network/parse failure → AuthUnavailable. OK.

Session resolution failure paths already exist (Unauthorized).

So ONE new code: `OauthStateUnknown` (401, title "OAuth state unknown or expired"). And `MalformedCallback`? Reuse MalformedAuth ("authorization header did not parse")? Title mismatch for query parse. Add `MalformedOAuth` (400, "OAuth callback did not parse"). Two new codes.

Table only grows ✓ statuses/codes append-only ✓ golden problem bodies: problem.rs golden tests lock specific emitted bodies per code — adding variants doesn't disturb existing locks (each golden asserts one code's body). Are there goldens enumerating ALL codes? "problem+json error bodies… locked by golden tests" — need checks: if there's a loop over all variants asserting shapes, new variants need new golden files. I'll check problem.rs golden test when writing.

Also constants: OAUTH_STATE_TTL_MS ✓, SESSION_TTL_MS ✓, prefixes ✓, cookie name ✓. New: nothing else needed except maybe OAUTH scopes string (module-local).

Entropy seam: the worker samples getrandom; core functions take `random: &[u8; 16]`.

Base64url encoding: cachet-crypto has no base64 module; worker uses base64ct directly. For core session-id formatting I need base64url-no-pad. Options: (1) add base64ct as a cachet-core dep — it's small, no_std-friendly; (2) hand-roll. base64ct is already in the tree (worker). crate boundary: core is "pure" — base64ct is pure computation ✓ fine with a // why comment.

