1	//! The cron-driven collector. cachet-core decides everything destructive
2	//! as data (the walk, the gates, the plan, the report); this module
3	//! enumerates, reads, walks, deletes, and reports against the real
4	//! bucket, persisting `meta/gc-cursor` at each boundary so a cache of any
5	//! size sweeps across ticks inside one binding's limits.
6	//!
7	//! Resume semantics: inventory and leases recompute every tick (their
8	//! listings are bounded and their outputs are cheap to rebuild), while
9	//! the mark, collect, and sweep stages hold frozen state in the cursor.
10	//! Frozen marks and a frozen plan only ever over-protect a path relative
11	//! to a newer inventory, which is the safe direction for a deletion
12	//! system; the grace boundary settles at plan time and stays settled for
13	//! the run.
14	//!
15	//! Budget accounting: every binding call spends one operation against
16	//! [`GC_OP_BUDGET`] (one list page, one read, one put, or one deletion
17	//! batch counts the same, because the batch is the API unit), and the
18	//! wall clock answers to [`GC_HEADROOM_MS`]. Either limit hit freezes the
19	//! run: the cursor already holds what the next tick needs, because
20	//! deletion is idempotent and ordered narinfos before NARs at every
